hange the resource, so it will refuse the attempt to consume logs. (the description says: "a mill saws it into slats") make leads basic: The graphite-stock law does not list this rule, so the law will block the attempt to use up graphite to make leads. (the description says: "mixed with clay into leads") make pencils basic: The slats-stock law does not include this rule, meaning it will refuse the factory's attempt to use up slats during pencil assembly. (the description says: "glues leads into slats") nothing in the rules: Leads are created at the lead maker, but there is no rule to transfer them to the factory, so the factory can never acquire them to assemble pencils. (the description says: "glues leads into slats") nothing in the rules: The shops receive delivered boxes of packed pencils, but the selling rule uses unboxed pencils, and there is no rule to unpack them. (the description says: "which sell them to customers") Fix each of these. In the 24-round test the engine refused these steps for breaking the world's own laws: make-leads-basic broke graphite-stock ("Graphite stock changes only when graphite is mined or bought."): make-leads changed the world's total graphite by -1; only mine-graphite, buy-graphite may change it; saw-slats-basic broke logs-stock ("Log stock changes only when cedar is cut or logs are bought."): saw-slats changed the world's total logs by -1; only cut-cedar, buy-logs may change it. For each: if the description says this step makes or uses up that resource (sawing logs uses logs, packing pencils uses loose pencils), the law is incomplete and the world design must list the step in that law's may_change_by; a process the world runs by itself must be listed as 'process:<its id>'. Otherwise the step is wrong: change it so the resource only moves between holders, or remove it. In the 24-round test these moves were never possible for anyone: buy-slats (The seller has a slat: was 0, needed 1; business.slats is raised only by saw-slats (on it